course: dictionary
lesson: 14320
title: Obliterated: destroyed or hidden completely
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Obliterate means to wipe out completely. The clues in the sentence tell you what is being wiped out. «The huge wave obliterated the little harbour town.» Nothing of the town is left standing, so obliterated means destroyed completely.
explain: «Fresh snow obliterated the footprints in the garden.» The snow covered the footprints so they could no longer be seen. This is the second meaning: covered or hidden, so that nothing can be seen. Ask yourself: is the thing gone, or just hidden?
explain: Sports writers use the word in a different, informal way. «Our team was obliterated 9-0 in the final.» The big score is the clue: it means beaten very badly. Check any meaning by putting it into the sentence. If «destroyed» or «hidden» makes sense, you have it.
fact: Obliterated is the past tense of the verb obliterate, and it can also work as an adjective.
fact: «Obliterated» means destroyed completely, so that nothing is left.
fact: «Obliterated» can also mean covered or hidden so that it cannot be seen.
fact: In sport, «obliterated» is informal for beaten very badly.
fact: The city of Pompeii was obliterated by a volcano, which is why people use this word when they talk about history.
exercise: «The flood obliterated the village, and nothing was left standing.» What does obliterated mean here? | Covered with a little dust | *Destroyed completely | Slowly cleaned | Painted in bright colours
exercise: «Rain had obliterated the chalk drawing on the pavement.» What does obliterated mean here? | Copied onto paper | Traced over carefully | *Hidden so that it could not be seen | Made brighter
exercise: «Our team was obliterated in the final, losing 8-0.» What does obliterated suggest? | Won very easily | Forgot the rules | Played to a draw | *Beaten very badly
test: «The tidal wave obliterated the little harbour town.» What does obliterated mean? | *Completely destroyed | Moved up into the hills | Brought back to life | Lightly decorated
test: Ella says, «The old words on the stone were obliterated by years of wind and rain.» What does it mean? | Copied into a book | *Worn away until they could no longer be seen | Read aloud by many people | Carved more deeply
exam: Which sentence uses obliterated to mean covered or hidden? | «The bomb obliterated the old bridge.» | «The volcano obliterated the village.» | *«Fresh snow obliterated the footprints on the path.» | «The team was obliterated by a huge score.»
